The standalone ruleset keeps every land and sea unit the engine can play, and their upgrades. Its filter (C7/Lua/standalone/ruleset.lua) kept only the 27 units with community art, and dropped every unit's upgrades: it looked the upgradesTo list up as a key of the art map, which is always nil. With no upgrades, no unit ever went obsolete (Rome could build Spearmen beside Pikemen at T200), 25 civs lost a whole unit class to their missing unique units (Rome had no Swordsman-class unit, France no Musketman), and the Industrial and Modern techs unlocked no unit at all. The filter now keeps 76 units: the 27, the 12 later land units (Rifleman to Modern Armor and Radar Artillery), the 7 later sea units (Ironclad to AEGIS Cruiser and the Transport) and the 30 unique units, each new one with the art folder of the unit it replaces or descends from, and their upgrades to units kept. It leaves out the air units, missiles and nukes, the Carrier, Nuclear Submarine, Flak and Mobile SAM (the engine has no air movement, air missions or nuclear attack: an air unit cannot even move), and the Leader, Army, Princess and named leaders (unproducible, with no rule behind them). The kept units' era and slave art variants are cleared, since they name folders this art does not have. UnitPrototype.GetUnitUpgrade, which logged a warning and took the alphabetically first target when several fit a civ (the Spearman lists both the Pikeman and, for the Dutch, the Musketman), now takes the next step: the cheapest target costing no less than the unit, then the cheapest, then by name; the chain stops at a repeat.

